}, {
  "caption": "Figure 1: Illustration of hierarchical representations of proteins. Different colors represent different types of amino acids. The filled circles are Cα atoms, and non-filled are other atoms. Each amino acid in the protein has different levels of inner structures. From coarse-grained to fine-grained levels of representations, we can use Cα coordinates, backbone atom coordinates, or all-atom coordinates to represent the protein structure. Note that we treat each amino acid as a node in the graph modeling despite different levels. By doing this, we can effectively make use of the inherent hierarchies within an amino acid. As a result, we achieve three hierarchical levels of representations for the protein. In addition, we obtain a graph with relatively small size as amino acids are usually much less than atoms in a protein. The actual atoms are in 3D, and this illustration uses 2D for simplicity.",
