}, {
  "caption": "Table 1: Comparisons of existing protein learning methods. Firstly, when modeling graphs, an amino acid or an atom is treated as a node in different methods. However, using atoms as nodes leads to high complexity. Here n, N , and k denote the number of amino acids, the number of atoms, and the average degree in a 3D protein graph, and N ≫ n. Note that the complexity also depends on the message passing scheme of each method as discussed in existing studies (Liu et al., 2022; Klicpera et al., 2021; Wang et al., 2022). In addition, most existing methods only capture one level of protein structures, and only IEConv (Hermosilla et al., 2021) considers hierarchical relations of proteins using several pooling layers. Our method can learn hierarchical representations at three levels. Lastly, our method can capture 3D structures completely at all levels.",
